{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Specification-driven development is a governed derivation and disambiguation method: current goals precede deeper repricing, intent precedes implementation, product definition precedes detailed requirements, and design explicitly bridges requirements to code.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":288,
          "quote":"The method commits to these (P2, P6). It defines the\ncurrent goals for the active body of work before repricing deeper\nconstitutional layers. It works from intent before implementation. It\ndefines the current product definition in explicit present-tense terms\nbefore decomposing it into detailed requirements. It defines the full\nconstitutional what in requirements, not merely a feature list. It prefers\ndeclaring desired truth and lawful boundaries over prescribing step-by-step\nimperative procedure. It makes design the explicit structural bridge\nbetween requirements and code. It requires code to be derivable from\nrequirements and design, not defended as accidental precedent. It treats\nspec-driven development as a disambiguation pipeline: each major gate\nreduces the space of lawful interpretations before downstream realisation\nproceeds.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FManifesto",
          "start_line":276
        }
      ],
      "key":"spec_driven_method",
      "label":"Specification-driven method"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Goals orient the current bounded work wave; they do not substitute for requirements.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":407,
          "quote":"Goals orient the current bounded work\nwave and are not a substitute requirement surface.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FSpecification%20surface%20rule",
          "start_line":406
        }
      ],
      "key":"goals_surface",
      "label":"Goals surface"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Intent states why the system exists and which directional change is in or out of scope.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":409,
          "quote":"Intent states why the\nsystem exists and what directional change is in or out of scope; it carries\nno optional realisation detail unless that detail is itself constitutional.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FSpecification%20surface%20rule",
          "start_line":407
        }
      ],
      "key":"intent_surface",
      "label":"Intent surface"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Product definition is the current concrete, present-tense bridge from intent to requirements, including lifecycle signal material to product meaning; it is not release notes.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":415,
          "quote":"Product states the current concrete product definition and bridges intent\nto requirements; it is not a release-note surface; it carries enough\noperational lifecycle signal for downstream requirements and design to\nunderstand intended use, release and install posture, live-use posture,\nobservability posture, and retirement or supersession posture where those\nmaterially affect product meaning.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FSpecification%20surface%20rule",
          "start_line":410
        }
      ],
      "key":"product_definition",
      "label":"Product definition"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Requirements state the full constitutional WHAT as stable obligations with explicit acceptance criteria, sufficient for downstream design derivation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":420,
          "quote":"Requirements state stable obligations\nthat must be true; they carry explicit acceptance criteria and remain\nsufficient for downstream design derivation, including lifecycle\nobligations or named lifecycle ambiguity gaps where build, assurance,\nrelease, deployment, live usage, telemetry or retirement materially affect\nthe obligation.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FSpecification%20surface%20rule",
          "start_line":415
        }
      ],
      "key":"requirements_surface",
      "label":"Requirements surface"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Design is the downstream structural HOW that realises requirement truth and chooses lawful mechanisms, structures, interfaces, carriers, packaging and tenant boundaries.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":423,
          "quote":"Design states how requirement truth is realised; it is\ndownstream of requirements and may choose structure, interfaces, carriers,\npackaging and tenant boundaries. ADRs are durable design memory, not a\nsecond requirement surface.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FSpecification%20surface%20rule",
          "start_line":420
        }
      ],
      "key":"design_surface",
      "label":"Design surface"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Code is executable realisation derived from requirements and design, rather than a source of authority through accidental precedent.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":285,
          "quote":"It requires code to be derivable from\nrequirements and design, not defended as accidental precedent.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FManifesto",
          "start_line":284
        }
      ],
      "key":"code_realisation",
      "label":"Code realisation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Reconstruction sufficiency is competent reconstruction of conformant downstream layers in order. It is not determinism and cannot depend on hiding major ambiguity.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":337,
          "quote":"The generic law, that a layered specification is sufficient when a competent\nactor can derive each layer from the one above, that sufficiency is not\ndeterminism, that a boundary crossed only by hiding a major ambiguity is not\nsufficient, and that an accepted relation may be reused on its exact basis\nafter checking applicability and invalidation, is EVALUATION §6.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FReconstruction%20litmus",
          "start_line":333
        }
      ],
      "key":"reconstruction_sufficiency",
      "label":"Reconstruction sufficiency"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Renewal is the cumulative feedback cycle from current specification through real-world use case and explicit gap analysis to lawful re-entry and forward repricing.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":380,
          "quote":"current specification → real-world use case → gap analysis → re-entry at the layer its change class names",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FRenewal%20path",
          "start_line":380
        }
      ],
      "key":"renewal_cycle",
      "label":"Renewal cycle"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["The tier-3 DESIGN_MODULE profile governs the boundary under its own adoption rule."],
      "definition":"The design profile's realisation shape makes meaning readable as transforms over admitted truth and locates effects explicitly at the edge.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":469,
          "quote":"For a realisation boundary that the design profile governs (DESIGN_MODULE,\ntier 3, adopted under its own adoption rule), the core rule is: semantic\nsteps should read as transforms over admitted truth; effects should be\nexplicit and pushed to the edge. If a reader has to inspect orchestration\ncode, mutable workspace state, service methods or fallback defaults to\ndetermine what the system means, the design is too imperative and too\ncoupled (P1, P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FRealisation%20shape",
          "start_line":463
        }
      ],
      "key":"profile_realisation_shape",
      "label":"Profile-governed realisation shape"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Requirement category and lifecycle status are independent declared-state domains. Categories are Capability (required behaviour/function/surface), Constraint / Guarantee (invariant, law, safety, portability or semantic bound), Governance (lifecycle, authority, visibility, release or operator policy), and Verification (tests, evidence, traceability, qualification or forensic obligations). Statuses are Active, Deferred, Superseded and Orphaned.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":492,
          "quote":"| Capability | a behaviour, function or surface the system must provide | what can the system do |\n| Constraint / Guarantee | an invariant, law, safety property, portability rule or semantic guarantee that bounds how the system may behave | what must always remain true |\n| Governance | a policy or control rule over lifecycle, authority, visibility, release or operator action | what governs use and change |\n| Verification | a requirement on tests, evidence, traceability, qualification or forensic surfaces | how do we know the claim is true |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FRequirement%20categories%20and%20classification",
          "start_line":489
        },
        {
          "end_line":509,
          "quote":"Every live requirement family is classified on two independent axes (P3,\nP1): lifecycle status (Active,\nDeferred, Superseded, Orphaned) and requirement category (the four above).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FRequirement%20categories%20and%20classification",
          "start_line":507
        }
      ],
      "key":"requirement_classifications",
      "label":"Requirement classifications"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Constitutional trace closure closes the constitution over realised behaviour and is stricter than documentation completeness.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":566,
          "quote":"Spec-driven development requires constitutional trace closure: the rule\nthat closes the constitution over realised behaviour, stricter than\ndocumentation completeness (P6, P4b).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FTrace%20closure%20and%20anti-drift",
          "start_line":564
        }
      ],
      "key":"constitutional_trace_closure",
      "label":"Constitutional trace closure"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["A domain artifact has been published as a live constitutional surface."],
      "definition":"A live domain surface is versioned constitutional history, immutable in place; a project may accumulate multiple such surfaces.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":610,
          "quote":"A project may accumulate multiple live domain surfaces over time. A live\nsurface is versioned constitutional history, not scratch space (P3). Engine code, design documents, tests and\ntooling may be refactored aggressively while they remain mutable\nimplementation surfaces. Live domain artifacts, once published as live\nconstitutional surfaces, are immutable in place; if one is wrong, the valid\nactions are to supersede it with a new version or to withdraw or delete it",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FLive%20surface%20immutability",
          "start_line":605
        }
      ],
      "key":"live_domain_surface",
      "label":"Live domain surface"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["The designed product, application, module, function, runtime surface, plugin surface, public interface, data surface or capability is material."],
      "definition":"The canonical operational lifecycle is intent, requirement, build, assurance, release, deployment, live usage, observed telemetry and retirement. A phase has one declared answer: answered; not applicable with reason; or a named Gap:/Unanswered: marker.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":627,
          "quote":"Any material\nproduct, application, module, function, runtime surface, plugin\nsurface, public interface, data surface or capability has an operational\nlifecycle, whose canonical chain is intent, requirement, build, assurance,\nrelease, deployment, live usage, observed telemetry, retirement.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FOperational%20lifecycle%20sufficiency",
          "start_line":623
        },
        {
          "end_line":648,
          "quote":"Each phase has one of three answers, and this\nsection owns them: the phase is answered by the surface; if a phase is\nnot applicable, the surface says why; if it is unknown, a named gap such\nas `Gap:` or `Unanswered:` is recorded.\nSilent absence is not a valid lifecycle answer.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FOperational%20lifecycle%20sufficiency",
          "start_line":644
        }
      ],
      "key":"operational_lifecycle",
      "label":"Operational lifecycle"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["The work wave bears a product outcome."],
      "definition":"A work wave binds one explicitly selected, unresolved, directly verifiable product-defined outcome instance and its declared acceptance interval.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":672,
          "quote":"Every product-outcome-bearing work wave identifies one explicitly selected,\nunresolved, directly verifiable product-defined outcome and declares the\nacceptance interval for that instance. Product-progress claims and\npromotion onto the supported product path are judged against that same\noutcome (P2; FOUNDATIONS §2\nOutcome U is the generic object).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F3.%20Outcome%20conservation",
          "start_line":667
        }
      ],
      "key":"selected_product_outcome",
      "label":"Selected product outcome"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Product-outcome evidence distinguishes material advance, prerequisite readiness, preservation and regression. These are declared classifications rather than evaluation core values.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":685,
          "quote":"Evidence distinguishes material advance, prerequisite readiness,\npreservation and regression. The four refine the progress\ncategories of [ACTIVATION §5](ACTIVATION.md#5-work-state-and-restart)\n(Progress) for a product outcome: material advance is progress there,\naccepted material movement toward the selected product outcome, and only\nmaterial advance projects product progress; prerequisite readiness is\nits prerequisite work, which names the product obligation it enables and\nhere also remains bounded; preservation is its preservation and repeated\nproof, useful evidence, not progress; regression maps to evidence that\nis not new progress, and this member adds one need to it: the promotion\nblock in the last paragraph of this section. Churn and the revision of the forecast are\nACTIVATION §5's, unchanged here.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F3.%20Outcome%20conservation",
          "start_line":674
        }
      ],
      "key":"outcome_evidence_classes",
      "label":"Outcome evidence classes"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["Within a product-outcome-bearing work wave."],
      "definition":"Material growth has a live basis in the selected unresolved outcome, an admitted named bounded prerequisite, or an admitted named bounded experiment whose stated observation discriminates a stated decision for that outcome.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":719,
          "quote":"Within a product-outcome-bearing work wave, material realisation growth\nderives its authority from the selected unresolved product outcome, an\nadmitted named bounded prerequisite to it, or an admitted named bounded\nexperiment whose stated observation discriminates a stated decision for it.\nA prerequisite or experiment authorises only its declared provisional\nbound; it does not select or enlarge the product outcome, authorise\ndownstream work, confer promotion or closure, or waive applicable design,\nsafety, authority or release law, including the prime law of a design\nprofile where one applies.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F3.%20Outcome%20conservation",
          "start_line":711
        }
      ],
      "key":"bounded_growth_basis",
      "label":"Bounded growth basis"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Change management routes reports through intake triage, owning-layer change classification, separation of computed treatment from judgment and owner decisions, and affected-span consistency closure.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":761,
          "quote":"For an arriving report, start at [Universal intake triage](#universal-intake-triage).\n[Change classes](#change-classes) locates the owning layer;\n[Computed classification and treatment](#computed-classification-and-treatment)\nseparates what rules decide from judgment and owner decisions;\n[Consistency gate](#consistency-gate) decides closure over the affected span.\nThe family table traces those rules and creates no second decision path.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management",
          "start_line":756
        }
      ],
      "key":"change_management",
      "label":"Change management"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"F11 is the foundation-to-law family accounting for change entry, computed classification and reuse, ambiguity governance and shared-interface migration. Its instance premises are changing specification, one front door, shared interfaces with multiple producers/consumers, and decisions either under declared policy or reserved to human authority. It reports P0 recovered; P1, P2, P3, P4a and P6 independent; P4b derived from P0/P2 with non-reacquirable step facts; and P5 derived from P0/P6 with an open-world premise.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":782,
          "quote":"This family (F11 in the index of\n[FOUNDATIONS §3 Foundation to law](FOUNDATIONS.md#foundation-to-law))\ndecides how a change enters the chain, how its classification is computed\nand reused, how ambiguity is governed and how a shared interface is\nmigrated. Its instance premises, which the commitments do not supply, are\nnamed in the rows as follows: changing specification, the specification\nchanges while work proceeds; one front door, reports of every kind arrive\nat one product; shared interfaces, a shared interface has several\nproducers and consumers; reserved decisions, a decision may be taken\nunder declared policy or be reserved to a human authority. Standing of\nthe commitments cited (FOUNDATIONS §3): P0 recovered; P1, P2, P3, P4a and\nP6 independent; P4b derived from P0 and P2, with P0's premise that\nstep-produced facts are not re-acquirable; P5 derived from P0 and P6,\nwith P0's open-world premise.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20change%20management%20and%20ambiguity",
          "start_line":769
        }
      ],
      "key":"foundation_law_family",
      "label":"F11 foundation-to-law family"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Universal intake triage is the single entry for substantive change, determining substance, affected product boundary/scope, lawful class, re-entry point, affected downstream surfaces/evidence, and release-scope fit or repricing.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":835,
          "quote":"Intake triage determines six things: whether the report represents a\nsubstantive change at all; the affected product boundary and intended\nscope; the lawful change class; the lawful re-entry point into the\nconstitutional chain; the downstream surfaces and evidence that must be\nrepriced, re-derived or re-proved, a determination whose evidence part is\nthe selection `S_Δ` over the impact cover `I(Δ)` of the affected surfaces\n([FOUNDATIONS §4 Change impact and evidence reuse](FOUNDATIONS.md#change-impact-and-evidence-reuse));\nand whether the work remains within the\ncurrently declared release scope or requires repricing of that release\nplan.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FUniversal%20intake%20triage",
          "start_line":826
        }
      ],
      "key":"intake_triage",
      "label":"Universal intake triage"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The minimum lawful declared change classes are goal_reprice, intent_reprice, product_reprice, requirement_reprice, design_reframe and realization_refactor. Each identifies changed meaning, lawful re-entry and downstream flow; a declared class is not an evaluation result.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":881,
          "quote":"| `goal_reprice` | current bounded work-wave focus changes | Goals | Intent, Product, Requirements, Design, Code and Evidence where that focus changes deeper constitutional truth |\n| `intent_reprice` | directional or scope change | Intent | Product, Requirements, Design, Code, Evidence |\n| `product_reprice` | current product realisation changes while directional intent stays stable | Product | Requirements, Design, Code, Evidence |\n| `requirement_reprice` | constitutional truth changes while project direction stays stable | Requirements | Design, Code, Evidence |\n| `design_reframe` | realisation structure changes while active intent and requirement truth stay stable | Design | Code, Evidence |\n| `realization_refactor` | local code, configuration or attribute change with no intended constitutional or structural change | the realised surface only | nothing upstream; it must prove no upstream drift |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FChange%20classes",
          "start_line":876
        }
      ],
      "key":"change_classes",
      "label":"Change classes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Reusable intake classification distinguishes C, reproducible facts and declared-rule application; J, residual semantic/materiality judgment; and O, a ruling reserved to its actual owner. These annotations create no authority, calculus kind, runtime, classifier interface or mandatory document.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":913,
          "quote":"Intake supplies one reusable classification result rather than requiring\neach role to reconstruct the same facts (P4b, P6, P1). Three annotations keep the kinds\nof determination apart: C denotes reproducible facts and declared rule\napplication; J denotes residual semantic or materiality judgment; O denotes\na ruling reserved to its actual owner. The annotations grant no authority,\nan instance of\n[FOUNDATIONS §2 Authority kinds](FOUNDATIONS.md#authority-kinds), What\ncreates no authority, and introduce no calculus kind, runtime, classifier interface or mandatory\ndocument.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FComputed%20classification%20and%20treatment",
          "start_line":905
        }
      ],
      "key":"computed_classification",
      "label":"Computed classification and C/J/O"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The consistency gate applies the externally owned condition-based closure rule to obligations of a declared change span, requiring upstream sufficiency, downstream consistency and evidence of active intended behaviour.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1019,
          "quote":"No gate closes while the affected active surfaces are internally\ninconsistent (P6, P4b). For the declared\nchange span the framework proves that the upstream surface remains\nsufficient for the downstream surface, that downstream artifacts do not\ncontradict active upstream truth, and that tests and qualification prove\nthe active intended behaviour rather than stale precedent. If that proof is\nmissing the change remains open even if one local artifact already looks\ncorrect.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FConsistency%20gate",
          "start_line":1012
        }
      ],
      "key":"consistency_gate",
      "label":"Consistency gate"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A sprint is a bounded execution-control surface under existing constitutional authority, allowing batching, coordination, proof-cost pricing and close review without becoming a constitutional layer.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1048,
          "quote":"A sprint is a bounded execution-control surface, not a new layer in the\nconstitutional chain (P6, P2). It may\nbatch work, coordinate tickets or iteration entries, price proof cost and\nforce close review under existing goals, intent, product definition,\nrequirements and design authority; it does not create, supersede, weaken\nor override that authority.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FSprint%20execution%20boundary",
          "start_line":1043
        }
      ],
      "key":"sprint_boundary",
      "label":"Sprint execution boundary"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Work-carrier selection follows required lifetime: durable ticket for cross-session identity; sprint-local entry for within-batch traceability only; run/return record for one activation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1091,
          "quote":"The carrier of a work item is selected for its required lifetime: a\ndurable ticket where the work needs identity across sessions; a\nsprint-local entry where its only value is traceability inside a batch; a\nrun or return record where the work lives and dies with one activation.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FCarriers",
          "start_line":1088
        }
      ],
      "key":"work_carrier_selection",
      "label":"Work-carrier selection"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
