      "definition":"urn:stdo:concept:ambiguity-governance:human-adjudication names admitted human judgment resolving declared ambiguity. Admission, not the performer's identity or participation elsewhere, makes the operation human adjudication.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1219,
          "quote":"Human adjudication\n(`urn:stdo:concept:ambiguity-governance:human-adjudication`) is explicit\nhuman judgment admitted by authority to resolve declared ambiguity where\npolicy or risk appetite requires it. That admission makes an operation\nhuman adjudication. A person who relays, records or carries out a step\ndoes not adjudicate by doing so, and a person who carries or decides a\ndeclared ambiguity where policy permits it, without that admission, acts\nunder the policy and recording that govern lawful probabilistic\nprocessing; who performs an operation does not decide its kind",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F5.%20Ambiguity%20governance%20and%20lifecycle",
