      "key":"core_interface",
      "label":"Core interface"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["A change alters a load-bearing shared contract, carrier, resolver, provider, projection law, closure law or other interface depended on by multiple surfaces."],
      "definition":"Constitutional migration replaces shared authority across the affected interface family; it is not ordinary incremental local patching.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1314,
          "quote":"Core interface changes are not ordinary local patches. Where a change\nalters a load-bearing contract, carrier, resolver, provider, projection\nlaw, closure law or other shared interface that multiple surfaces depend\non, the work is handled as a constitutional migration, not as incremental\npatching (P3, P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FCore%20interface%20migration",
          "start_line":1310
        }
      ],
      "key":"constitutional_migration",
      "label":"Constitutional migration"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Two lawful strategies are inside-out hard-break migration on the same implementation line, replacing authoritative source truth from its carrier outward, and fundamental re-adoption for intentional major re-derivation on a materially different implementation basis. Bounded evolution is a rebuttable selection presumption, not a third strategy.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1342,
          "quote":"Core interface and major implementation replacement work has two lawful\nconstitutional strategies (P3, P6).\nInside-out hard-break migration applies when the project remains on the\nsame implementation line and the authoritative source truth is being\nreplaced in place; the work proceeds from the source carrier outward\nthrough a sequence of deliberate breaks and repairs. Fundamental\nre-adoption migration applies when the rewrite is major and the project is\nintentionally re-deriving itself on a materially different implementation\nbasis, such as a new runtime model, carrier model, type system, execution\nsubstrate or realisation tree; the prior implementation is moved sideways\nand treated as reference material, not live authority.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FConstitutional%20migration%20options",
          "start_line":1332
        }
      ],
      "key":"migration_strategies",
      "label":"Constitutional migration strategies"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"An affected outcome's acceptance path is its full causal closure: every admitted entrypoint, producer, consumer, resolver, fallback, shared state, event, projection and proof surface capable of changing, interpreting or closing it.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1383,
          "quote":"For this rule the affected acceptance path is the full causal closure of\nthe outcome: every admitted entrypoint, producer, consumer, resolver,\nfallback, shared state surface, event, projection and proof surface capable\nof changing, interpreting or closing the outcome. Singular authority means\none governing authority across that closure, not one selected successful\ntrace. If the same request, fact, identity, state or projection is\nadmissible to old and new authority, authority is competing and promotion\nblocks (P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FConstitutional%20migration%20options",
          "start_line":1376
        }
      ],
      "key":"acceptance_path",
      "label":"Affected acceptance path"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Projections, reports, status views and topology views are downstream reflections of an authoritative carrier, not independent closure surfaces.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1441,
          "quote":"Projections, reports, status views and topology views may reflect the\nauthoritative carrier; they do not become a second closure surface (P6,\nP1).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FProjection%20discipline",
          "start_line":1439
        }
      ],
      "key":"projection_read_model",
      "label":"Projection read model"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["The project intentionally abandons its prior constitutional shape and re-derives on a new branch, version line or methodology basis."],
      "definition":"Fundamental migration is a constitutional reset through explicit adoption. Inherited constitutional material is classified adopted, superseded, deferred or orphaned; implementation is carry_across, redundant or rewrite.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1534,
          "quote":"A fundamental migration is a constitutional reset, not an in-place\nrefactor. It applies when a project intentionally abandons a prior\nconstitutional shape and re-derives itself on a new branch, version line or\nmethodology basis (P3, P6). In that case:\ninherited constitutional documents from the prior line become migration\nsource material, not live authority in the new line; it is lawful to zero\ninherited constitutional surfaces on the migration line and rebuild them\nfrom first principles; nothing carries forward by default, and every\nretained statement, requirement, design choice or guide rule must be",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FFundamental%20migration",
          "start_line":1526
        }
      ],
      "key":"fundamental_migration",
      "label":"Fundamental migration"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A transformation wave describes refactor/migration over mutable implementation surfaces, with bounded non-authoritative transitional plumbing during the wave and one current operative surface for its affected scope when it lands.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1609,
          "quote":"Refactor and migration should be understood as a transformation wave over\nmutable\nimplementation surfaces (P3, P6). While\nthe wave is in flight: temporary mixed-state implementation may exist only\nbelow the current break boundary and only as explicitly non-authoritative\nplumbing; transitional adapters or scaffolds may exist only when they are\nnamed, bounded and outside the acceptance path; refactor state may still\ncarry traces of the prior operative model, never as competing or ambiguous\ndual authority over the same request, fact, identity, state or projection.\nExplicitly specified, deterministically routed, non-overlapping product or\ncompatibility scopes are not competing authority under this rule; they may\ncoexist while the enclosing migration remains visibly open.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FTransformation%20wave",
          "start_line":1598
        }
      ],
      "key":"transformation_wave",
      "label":"Transformation wave"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["Construction is delegated to a probabilistic or agentic worker."],
      "definition":"The method applies ACTIVATION's bounded construction step: accepted basis, affected relation set, selected computations, delegated execution/decision authority, construction/self-review, independent assessment where required, owner disposition accept/local_repair/re_enter/reject, exact accepted checkpoint and next already-authorised action. Candidate availability, assessment and owner disposition are separate results.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1630,
          "quote":"Construction delegated to a probabilistic or agentic worker is governed as\nthe step of ACTIVATION: the execution relation from accepted governing\nbasis through bounded affected relation set, selected computational\nrelations, delegated execution and decision authority, construction and\nself-review, independent live-surface assessment where required, one of\naccept, local repair, re-enter or reject, the exact accepted checkpoint,\nand the next already-authorised bounded action (ACTIVATION §1, §3, §5 and\n§7). The",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F8.%20Bounded%20execution%20in%20this%20method",
