    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"An ADR is durable design memory per decision boundary: the choice making acceptance criteria true. Its declared statuses are active, superseded and retired; it is an authoritative design file, while templates and optional registries project its rules.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1718,
          "quote":"Each ADR explicitly includes `Status:` (`active`, `superseded` or\n`retired`); `Implements:` (the requirement identifiers the ADR makes true);\n`Derives from:` (the intent identifier or strategy document that motivated\nthe decision); `Supersedes:` (the prior ADR or doctrine it replaces);\n`Superseded by:` (the successor ADR when its status is superseded); and\n`Retained special case:` (when earlier behaviour is intentionally retained\nas a special case of the current surface) (P6, P4b).\nADRs are written per decision boundary, not per requirement file; the\nboundary is the question \"what design choice makes these acceptance\ncriteria true\".",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F10.%20Design%20records",
