  "constraints": [
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["spec_driven_method"],
      "conditions": [],
      "exceptions":["Transitional paths may remain as explicitly retained compatibility features."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":299,
          "quote":"It demands evidence for claims through scenarios, tests, events,\nprojection and delta. It treats repricing as part of correctness: when\nreality exposes a constitutional gap, the specification changes. It keeps\nauthority directional while iterating: goals govern intent, intent governs\nproduct definition, product definition governs requirements, requirements\ngovern design, and requirements plus design govern code. It keeps the live\noperative surface in the present tense only: once a new current reality is\nestablished, transitional paths are erased from the live product unless\nexplicitly retained as compatibility features. It treats derived artifacts,\nindexes, trace matrices, reports, automation and generated views, as read\nmodels, never as constitutional truth. If these claims do not hold, the\nwork is not spec-driven.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FManifesto",
          "start_line":288
        }
      ],
      "key":"manifesto_commitments",
      "statement":"The method requires full constitutional requirements, code derivable from requirements/design, narrowing at major gates, evidence through scenarios/tests/events/projection/delta, and specification repricing when reality exposes a constitutional gap. Authority stays directional during iteration. Current operative truth is present-tense; obsolete transitions are erased subject to the compatibility exception. Derived artifacts, indexes, trace matrices, reports, automation and generated views are read models, never constitutional truth. Failure of these commitments excludes the spec-driven designation. Declarative truth/boundaries over imperative procedure remains a preference.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["spec_driven_method"],
      "conditions":["Any listed failure holds."],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":329,
          "quote":"The work is not spec-driven if any of these holds (P2, P6):\n\n1. Given goals, a competent team cannot derive a conformant intent surface.\n2. Given intent, a competent team cannot derive a conformant\n   product-definition surface.\n3. Given product definition, a competent team cannot derive conformant\n   requirements.\n4. Given requirements, a competent team cannot derive a conformant design.\n5. Given requirements and design, a competent team cannot derive conformant\n   code.\n6. A live requirement has no explicit status, category or owning design\n   decision.\n7. A live requirement has no downstream realisation or explicit deferment\n   surface.\n8. Code behaviour exists without requirement and design authority.\n9. A live domain artifact is rewritten in place after becoming part of the\n   live constitutional surface.\n10. A capability claim has no operational evidence.\n11. Drift is discovered, [Universal intake triage](#universal-intake-triage)\n    locates its cause in the constitutional source, and that source is not\n    repriced.\n12. A major ambiguity at a constitutional or realisation boundary is neither\n    recorded nor explicitly governed.\n13. A material product, module, function, application, runtime surface or\n    capability has no declared operational lifecycle signal and no recorded\n    gap for unanswered release, deployment, live-use, telemetry or\n    retirement questions.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FSpec-drivenness%20litmus",
          "start_line":303
        }
      ],
      "key":"spec_drivenness_exclusions",
      "statement":"Work is not spec-driven if a competent team cannot derive conformant intent from goals, product definition from intent, requirements from product, design from requirements, or code from requirements plus design; if any live requirement lacks explicit status, category, owning design decision, or both downstream realisation and explicit deferment; if code lacks requirement/design authority; if a live constitutional domain artifact is rewritten in place; if a capability lacks operational evidence; if triage attributes discovered drift to constitutional source that is not repriced; if major boundary ambiguity is neither recorded nor explicitly governed; or if a material product/module/function/application/runtime surface/capability has neither declared lifecycle signal nor recorded gap for unanswered release, deployment, live-use, telemetry or retirement questions.",
      "uncertainty":"These are exclusion conditions, not an independently sufficient acceptance test."
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["reconstruction_sufficiency"],
      "conditions": [],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":360,
          "quote":"A failure at any boundary is a specification defect of the upper layer. If\nintent cannot be derived from goals, the goals surface is unclear,\ncontradictory or not concrete enough to orient the next bounded wave of\nwork. If product cannot be derived from intent, the intent surface is too\nvague, contradictory or not concrete enough to guide realisation. If\nrequirements cannot be derived from product, the product surface is\nunderspecified, contradictory or not decomposable enough to govern\nimplementation. If design cannot be derived from requirements, the\nrequirement surface is underspecified, contradictory or polluted with\naccidental implementation detail. If code cannot be derived from\nrequirements plus design, the design surface is incomplete, ambiguous or\nnot operational enough. ADRs and design documents are therefore not\ndecorative explanation; they are the load-bearing bridge between\nconstitutional truth and executable realisation.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FReconstruction%20litmus",
          "start_line":347
        }
      ],
      "key":"reconstruction_boundary_defects",
      "statement":"Specification-drivenness requires ordered reconstructability. Boundary failure is an upper-layer defect: goals unclear/contradictory/insufficiently concrete for the bounded wave; intent vague/contradictory/insufficiently concrete for realisation; product underspecified/contradictory/insufficiently decomposable; requirements underspecified/contradictory/polluted by accidental implementation detail; or design incomplete/ambiguous/insufficiently operational for code. ADRs/design documents are load-bearing. Sufficiency depends on ambiguity governance; accepted relations may be reused only on exact basis after applicability/invalidation checks. A local selected function's span excludes rebuilding the whole product. The litmus neither waives the design profile's material-change gate nor requires recreation of unchanged accepted views.",
      "uncertainty":"Detailed sufficiency, reconstruction-span and design-profile rules are externally owned."
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["renewal_cycle"],
      "conditions":["A real use case, including scenario, deployment or external review, pressures the current model."],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":393,
          "quote":"The generative rule: a real use case (scenario, deployment, external review)\nhits the current model; gap analysis identifies what the constitution cannot\nexpress; if the gap is constitutional, not merely a missing implementation,\nit re-enters at the layer its [change class](#change-classes) names, and\nnew goals or intent are written where it changes the work wave, direction\nor scope; the repriced surface then flows forward through the layers that\nclass lists. New intents emerge from repeated, real use-case pressure\nagainst the current model, not from abstract speculation; a gap becomes\nintent only once it is concrete. Ad hoc coding pressure does not generate\nintent; explicit gap analysis does. This is how the system stays alive\ninstead of becoming a frozen constitution.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FRenewal%20path",
          "start_line":383
        }
      ],
      "key":"renewal_reentry",
      "statement":"Explicit gap analysis identifies what the constitution cannot express. A constitutional gap, distinguished from missing implementation, re-enters at its change class's layer and flows forward through that class's layers. New goals/intent arise only where work-wave focus, direction or scope changes; intent emerges from concrete, repeated real-use pressure, not abstract speculation or ad hoc coding pressure. Requirements/design refine cumulatively, code is repeatedly re-derived, and implementation/testing/replay/scenario gaps feed repricing without reversing authority.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["goals_surface","intent_surface","product_definition","requirements_surface","design_surface"],
      "conditions": [],
      "exceptions":["Optional realisation detail may appear in intent when itself constitutional.","Explicit version labels are reserved for immutable release facts, selector bindings, or clearly historical/superseded material."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":441,
          "quote":"Constitutional specification is current-surface law and is written in the\npresent tense. Dead law does not remain in active constitutional artifacts\nfor historical comfort: what is no longer live is deleted, superseded or\nmoved to commentary, and a migration wave does not justify preserving stale\nconstitutional wording inside the live surface (P3). During a change wave\nthe live constitutional surface is written in terms of the current truth\nand the declared affected boundary; version-line branding such as `2.0` or\n`3.0` is not used merely to indicate recency, and explicit version labels\nare reserved for immutable release facts, selector bindings, or clearly\nhistorical or superseded material (§6).\n\nBefore a downstream specification artifact is treated as complete, the\nwhole affected span is checked: is the change class clear; is the re-entry\npoint lawful for that class; can each downstream layer still be derived\nfrom the upstream layer; do the active intent, product, requirements,\ndesign and qualification surfaces still say the same thing in the affected\nscope. If not, the gate is not closed (§4, consistency gate).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FSpecification%20surface%20rule",
          "start_line":425
        }
      ],
      "key":"current_surface_and_span",
      "statement":"Preserve each layer's role: goals do not replace requirements, intent excludes optional nonconstitutional implementation detail, product is not release notes, requirements carry explicit acceptance and material lifecycle obligations/gaps, and ADRs are not a second requirements surface. Live constitutional wording is present-tense current truth at its declared affected boundary; dead law is deleted, superseded or moved to commentary, even during migration. Recency alone does not justify version branding. Before treating a downstream artifact as complete, check the whole affected span for clear class, lawful re-entry, layer derivability and agreement among active intent/product/requirements/design/qualification; otherwise the gate stays open.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["requirements_surface","design_surface"],
      "conditions": [],
      "exceptions":["Upstream constitutional constraints bind choices; constitutional necessity may require specific filenames, placement, paths or packaging."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":459,
          "quote":"Requirements state what control or delivery surfaces must exist; design\ndecides the concrete mechanism that realises them. If a technology stack,\ncarrier or runtime is not already constrained by an upstream constitutional\nsurface, choosing it is part of design, not a separate upstream authority\n(P2, P1). A tenant's choice of stack, human process or\ncombination is its execution binding, declared in that design under §11.\nThe boundary matters especially for\nagent-facing bootstrap and control surfaces: requirements may say that\nauthoritative bootstrap or control surfaces must be present, carry bare\naxioms and route to the canonical method; design decides whether those\nsurfaces are entry documents, embedded blocks, standalone compiled\nartifacts or another lawful topology (PRODUCT_DEFINITION §12). If\nfilenames and placement are hardcoded in requirements without\nconstitutional necessity, the requirement surface has absorbed design\ndetail and made re-derivation harder.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FDesign%20rule",
          "start_line":445
        }
      ],
      "key":"design_choice_boundary",
      "statement":"Requirements specify required control/delivery surfaces; design chooses mechanisms, including stack/carrier/runtime and tenant execution binding unless upstream already constrains them. Agent-facing requirements may require authoritative bootstrap/control surfaces carrying bare axioms and routing to the canonical method; design chooses entry documents, embedded blocks, compiled artifacts or other lawful topology. Hardcoding filenames/placement without constitutional necessity absorbs design detail and impairs re-derivation.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["profile_realisation_shape"],
      "conditions":["The design profile governs the realisation boundary."],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":479,
          "quote":"Non-goals of that profile (P1, P4a): it does not require one specific programming language,\nelimination of all imperative code, monadic or category-theory vocabulary,\nor replacing every exception with a custom algebraic type. It does require\nthat the implementation shape remain readable, modular and honest about\nwhere meaning lives and where effects happen. Where a boundary composes\nhigher-order functions or effectful computation, it does require an\nexplicit composition and effect algebra with its applicable identity,\nclosure, cardinality, effect and authority-conservation laws (FOUNDATIONS\n§4 Composition). The vocabulary is optional; the law is not.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FRealisation%20shape",
          "start_line":471
        }
      ],
      "key":"realisation_shape_obligations",
      "statement":"Semantic steps should read as transforms over admitted truth, with explicit effects pushed to the edge. Meaning requiring inspection of orchestration, mutable workspace state, service methods or fallback defaults indicates excessive imperative coupling. Readability, modularity and honesty about meaning/effects are required. A boundary composing higher-order functions or effectful computation requires explicit composition/effect algebra with applicable identity, closure, cardinality, effect and authority-conservation laws.",
      "uncertainty":"The applicable algebra and profile-adoption rule are referenced but not fully supplied."
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["requirements_surface","requirement_classifications"],
      "conditions": [],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":525,
          "quote":"Every inherited or legacy requirement is classified as one of these, and\nnothing live remains unclassified (P3):\n\n| Classification | Meaning | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Deferred | retained in the live surface as an explicit not-yet-operative or not-yet-realised obligation | keep with an honest deferment surface; do not present as current closure |\n| Superseded | ownership has moved to a newer constitutional surface or design decision | add Implements and Supersedes to the governing design record |\n| Active | remains live constitutional law in the current surface | has an owning design decision; write or update one |\n| Orphaned | no longer part of the intended system | remove or explicitly supersede |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FRequirement%20categories%20and%20classification",
          "start_line":517
        }
      ],
      "key":"requirement_classification_rules",
      "statement":"Every live requirement family has explicit header Status and Category; either missing makes it incomplete. All four categories remain constitutional, not priority or user-facing labels. Scenarios validate capability meaning; not every requirement should become a marketed feature. Requirements should avoid premature concrete realisation choices; nonconstitutional path/filename/packaging choices belong to design. Every inherited/legacy requirement is classified: Deferred remains explicitly not-yet-operative/realised with honest deferment and no current-closure claim; Superseded transfers ownership and requires Implements/Supersedes in its governing design record; Active requires an owning design decision to be written/updated; Orphaned is removed or explicitly superseded. Nothing live remains unclassified.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["requirement_classifications","outcome_evidence_classes","change_classes","adr"],
      "conditions": [],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":544,
          "quote":"The lifecycle statuses (Active, Deferred, Superseded, Orphaned), the\ncategories and the legacy classifications are declared states of a\nrequirement, not results, and map to no core value. So are the other\ndeclared classes of this member: the kinds of evidence of §3 (material\nadvance, prerequisite readiness, preservation, regression), the old-path\nclasses of §7 Required migration protocol, the inherited-material\nclasses adopted, superseded, deferred and orphaned and the\nimplementation classifications `carry_across`, `redundant` and `rewrite`\nof §7 Fundamental migration, and the ADR `Status:` values of §10; the change\nclasses are declared states under §4 Change classes. Each records\na declared classification of its subject, not the answer to a predicate\nthat an evaluation decides. An evaluation that reads them returns its own\ncore value with its predicate under\n[FOUNDATIONS §4 Results](FOUNDATIONS.md#results). Purpose and material\nrelation: a declared state is never combined as an evaluation result, so\na requirement marked Active, or evidence classed as material advance,\ncannot stand in for a `satisfied` result, and a requirement marked\nDeferred or Orphaned cannot stand in for a `falsified` one (P5, P1).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FRequirement%20categories%20and%20classification",
          "start_line":527
        }
      ],
      "key":"declared_states_not_results",
      "statement":"Requirement lifecycle/category/legacy classifications, evidence kinds, migration old-path classes, inherited-material classes, carry_across/redundant/rewrite, ADR statuses and change classes are declared states with no core-value mapping. They are never combined as evaluation results; evaluations return their own predicate-specific core value. Active/material advance does not mean satisfied; Deferred/Orphaned does not mean falsified.",
      "uncertainty":"The complete core-result algebra is outside the supplied source."
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["requirement_classifications","fundamental_migration"],
      "conditions":["The project evolves a released predecessor as a successor release."],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":560,
          "quote":"The legacy classification answers one question: what standing an\ninherited requirement holds in the project's own live requirement\nsurface. The disposition of a predecessor claim under\n[RELEASE §9](RELEASE.md#9-successor-baseline-conservation-stdo-up-015)\nanswers another: what a successor release does with a claim of the\nreleased predecessor it evolves, a claim that qualification evaluates.\nWhere a project's work is also such a successor, each predecessor claim\ntakes its RELEASE §9 disposition by that section's predicates, and no\nlifecycle status or legacy classification supplies or replaces it: a\nrequirement marked Active may still be `superseded` where its meaning\nchanged, Superseded names no accepted successor relation, and Orphaned\nis not an intentional removal. Purpose and material relation: two\nvocabularies with overlapping words would otherwise let a project-level\nrecord stand in for a successor disposition that its owner has not made\n(P6, P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FRequirement%20categories%20and%20classification",
          "start_line":546
        }
      ],
      "key":"successor_disposition_separation",
      "statement":"Each predecessor claim requires its own RELEASE §9 disposition by that owner's predicates. Project lifecycle, legacy and migration-adoption classifications cannot supply or replace it. Active may coexist with successor superseded if meaning changed; Superseded establishes no accepted successor relation; Orphaned is not intentional removal.",
      "uncertainty":"RELEASE §9 predicates and disposition domain are not supplied."
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["constitutional_trace_closure","requirements_surface","design_surface","code_realisation"],
      "conditions": [],
      "exceptions":["A live requirement may have an explicit honest deferment surface instead of realised design/code/tests."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":580,
          "quote":"Ownership: every live requirement family maps to one or more owning design\ndecisions; every ADR or other design record grounds itself in requirements\nthrough an explicit `Implements:` line.\n\nDownstream closure: no live requirement remains a free-floating statement\nof intent; if it is live it is realised through the downstream chain or\nexplicitly deferred with an honest surface that records the deferment, so\nevery live requirement family has downstream closure, realised in design,\ncode and tests or explicitly deferred. No shipping code or tests exist as\nungoverned behaviour: behaviour that exists traces back to live\nrequirements and the design decisions that authorise it. No hidden product\nsurface is allowed; behaviour without trace authority is accidental law\neven if it works.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FTrace%20closure%20and%20anti-drift",
          "start_line":568
        }
      ],
      "key":"trace_ownership_and_drift",
      "statement":"Every live requirement family has one or more owning design decisions; every ADR/design record explicitly grounds requirements through Implements:. Live requirements close through design/code/tests or explicit honest deferment. All shipping code/tests trace to live requirements and authorising design; working untraced behaviour is still accidental law. Unowned requirements, ungrounded design, unrecorded deferment and ownerless code are drift; implementation-habit tests lock it in. Persistent event/projection delta is diagnosed by intake among code, requirement/design stack, predictive model or observation. Capability without a scenario has unverified operational meaning. Treating guarantees/governance/verification as features misclassifies them; in-place live-constitution correction corrupts history. A real-use gap inexpressible in requirements re-enters at Requirements or upstream according to class, not by a code hack.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["live_domain_surface"],
      "conditions": [],
      "exceptions":["Mutable implementation surfaces may be aggressively refactored while they retain that status.","Retained superseded responsibility must satisfy the migration retention relation."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":618,
          "quote":"Transitional implementation paths, migration\nscaffolds and fallback behaviours have no permanent authority in the live\nsurface: once superseded they are retired, and any retained responsibility\nsatisfies the retention relation of §7 (closure criterion). The past is\npreserved by version control, operational event history where present and\nsuperseded constitutional artifacts. The method does not require shipping\ncompatibility shims forever, and it does not allow silent mutation of live\nconstitutional history.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FLive%20surface%20immutability",
          "start_line":611
        }
      ],
      "key":"live_surface_change_boundary",
      "statement":"Published live constitutional domain artifacts cannot be silently mutated in place. An incorrect artifact is superseded by a new version or withdrawn/deleted from the live surface. Superseded transitions, scaffolds and fallbacks retire unless retention is justified; they have no permanent authority and compatibility shims need not ship forever. History is preserved by version control, operational events where present and superseded artifacts.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["operational_lifecycle","product_definition","requirements_surface","design_surface"],
      "conditions":["Lifecycle aspects materially affect product meaning or an obligation."],
      "exceptions":["No implementation mechanism for every phase and no release plan for every requirement is demanded."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":654,
          "quote":"For product definition this means naming the current product boundary,\nintended use context, release and install posture, live-use posture,\nobservability posture, and retirement or supersession posture where those\nmaterially affect the product's meaning. For requirements it means stating\nany invariant obligation that governs build, assurance, release,\ndeployment, live usage, telemetry, retirement, ownership, source truth,\nauthority boundary or downstream interpretation; a requirement may defer a\nphase only by naming the deferment and the surface that will own its later\nresolution.\n\nThe rule is an ambiguity detector, not a demand that every requirement\nbecome a release plan. Each phase has one of three answers, and this\nsection owns them: the phase is answered by the surface; if a phase is\nnot applicable, the surface says why; if it is unknown, a named gap such\nas `Gap:` or `Unanswered:` is recorded.\nSilent absence is not a valid lifecycle answer.\n[ASSURANCE §16](ASSURANCE.md#16-module-lifecycle-confirmation) applies\nthe three answers to the design evidence of a governed boundary. When the lifecycle signal is\nmissing, downstream design may not fill the gap by local convention,\nimplementation precedent, prompt prose or test fixture; it reprices the\nappropriate constitutional surface or carries the ambiguity as explicit,\ngoverned debt (§5).",
