    {
      "applies_to_keys":["method_procedures"],
      "conditions":["Bootstrapping a project or repricing requirements; alternatively, handling a real-use gap as specified."],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1155,
          "quote":"When bootstrapping a project or repricing a requirement surface: resolve\nthe applicable product definition, verify its exact installed basis, load\nits declared bootstrap entrypoint (PRODUCT_DEFINITION §12), then load the\ngoals surface or the bound equivalent current work-wave carrier; perform\nthe goals-to-intent step and write or confirm the intent surface; perform\nthe intent-to-product step and write or confirm the product definition as\nthe current bridge; gather requirement source material from the relevant\nconstitutional inputs; perform the product-to-requirements step and write\nthe resulting live surface under the requirement surface; store\nrequirements as individual files or grouped families, whichever best\npreserves clarity and avoids monolithic sprawl; make the requirement\nsurface the sole live requirement authority before proceeding to design and\ncode; prefer declarative structure over procedural workaround while shaping\nthe new surface; record any major ambiguity that remains at the current\nboundary before downstream realisation proceeds; only after that surface\nexists should downstream design and implementation be treated as\nconstitutionally grounded.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FProcedures",
          "start_line":1139
        },
        {
          "end_line":1170,
          "quote":"When a real use case reveals a gap: enter\n[Universal intake triage](#universal-intake-triage) first and keep the\nclassification unresolved until the evidence supports a lawful change class;\nthen write the scenario as the first\nsubstantive project artifact for the gap so the pressure becomes concrete\nand testable; run gap analysis to locate its cause under\n[Universal intake triage](#universal-intake-triage), which keeps open what\nthe evidence cannot yet separate; then re-enter at the layer the selected\n[change class](#change-classes) names and flow forward from it, writing new\ngoals or intent only where the work wave, direction or scope changes.\nThe intake source does not change this rule: a bug report, feature request,\nfailed test case, release blocker or operator observation still enters\nthrough intake triage first, then follows the lawful change class selected\nthere.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F4.%20Change%20management%2FProcedures",
          "start_line":1157
        }
      ],
      "key":"bootstrap_and_gap_procedures",
      "statement":"Bootstrap/repricing resolves product, verifies exact installed basis, loads declared bootstrap then goals/equivalent work-wave carrier, derives/confirms intent then product, gathers constitutional inputs and derives the live requirements surface. Individual files or grouped families are chosen for clarity without monolithic sprawl. Requirements become sole live requirement authority before design/code; declarative structure is preferred and remaining major ambiguity recorded before downstream realisation. Only then is downstream work treated as grounded. For a real-use gap, triage first leaves classification unresolved until evidence supports a class; scenario is the first substantive project artifact for the gap, followed by cause analysis preserving unresolved alternatives, selected re-entry and forward flow. New goals/intent require changed wave/direction/scope. Bug, feature, failed test, release blocker and operator origins do not bypass this sequence.",
      "uncertainty":"These procedures are represented as source data and have not been performed."
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["ambiguity_governance","lawful_probabilistic_processing","human_adjudication"],
      "conditions": [],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1206,
          "quote":"Major ambiguity is always surfaced and governed; it may\nnot remain hidden inside informal operator judgment, ambient precedent or\nsilent model choice. At each major boundary the process detects and\nrecords the major ambiguity that remains or is newly introduced; identifies\nthe affected invariant, asset or decision boundary; records the decision\ntaken if work proceeds; and records whether the ambiguity was resolved,\ncarried forward, escalated or blocked. Those outcomes are `resolved`,\n`carried`, `escalated` and `blocked` of\n[ACTIVATION §3 Ambiguity outcome](ACTIVATION.md#ambiguity-outcome), which\ngives each its core value for the predicate \"the declared ambiguity is\nresolved on the exact basis\". Only `resolved` is `satisfied`, so a later\nboundary rests no dependent claim on an ambiguity that is carried,\nescalated or blocked as if it were resolved. Ambiguity detection is\nmandatory; blocking is policy (P6, P5).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F5.%20Ambiguity%20governance%20and%20lifecycle",
          "start_line":1193
        }
      ],
      "key":"ambiguity_recording_and_outcomes",
      "statement":"At every major boundary detect/record remaining or new major ambiguity, affected invariant/asset/decision boundary, decision if work proceeds, and resolved/carried/escalated/blocked outcome. No hidden major ambiguity in informal judgment, precedent or silent model choice. For the exact-basis resolution predicate only resolved maps to satisfied; dependent claims cannot treat carried/escalated/blocked as resolved. Detection is mandatory; blocking follows declared risk policy subject to hard stops. Profile runtime shorthands remain in their own contexts absent explicit import/translation. A missing lifecycle answer is a recorded gap, never silence.",
      "uncertainty":"Core mappings of carried, escalated and blocked and the hard-stop classes are referenced but not supplied."
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["lineage_release"],
      "conditions": [],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1279,
          "quote":"Layer and release separation holds\n([AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS §7](AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#ac-020-layer-and-release-separation)):\na calculus, its interpretation of a subject and its carrier encoding keep\nindependent content identities, judgments, supersession relations and\nproof obligations, and none is automatically an independently released\nproduct. One constitutional surface and its version boundary for a project\nare\n[PRODUCT_DEFINITION §11](PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#11-one-constitutional-surface-and-version-boundary).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F6.%20Lineage%20and%20release",
          "start_line":1272
        }
      ],
      "key":"release_identity_and_layer_separation",
      "statement":"Recursive/compositional building does not merge lineage identities: product definition is source-project present truth, not released artifact or builder. Only immutable v<version>-rc.<n> is exact operative release identity; the mutable line selector is neither exact dependency, evidence nor constitutional basis and cannot silently alter consumer selection. Cut identity/accepted feature boundary belongs to release metadata/evaluation rather than live specification. Shared realisation surfaces should express current truth by role/boundary/status rather than version line. Release mechanics and successor conservation remain RELEASE-owned. Calculus, subject interpretation and carrier encoding retain independent content identities, judgments, supersession and proof obligations; none automatically becomes an independently released product.",
      "uncertainty":"Full lineage definitions, release process, successor conservation and project version-boundary rules are external."
    },
