    {
      "applies_to_keys":["constitutional_migration"],
      "conditions":["A core interface changes."],
      "exceptions":["Bounded product-slice proof remains subject to the path-relative promotion rule."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1328,
          "quote":"change the authoritative core contract first; ban bridge code for that\ncontract family as an authoritative surface; audit every producer and every\nconsumer of the impacted interface; migrate each producer and consumer to\nthe new contract; chase every downstream effect until no superseded closure\nlaw remains; run complete migration-closure proof only after the migration\nwave is complete, bounded product-slice proof remaining governed by the\npath-relative promotion rule below.\n\nThe rule exists because partial interface migration creates recurring\ndrift: one producer writes the new contract while another still writes the\nold; runtime reads one carrier while reporting or topology reads another;\ncompatibility aliases silently remain authoritative; projections become a\nsecond truth surface; tests go green while the architecture remains split.\nDeclaring such a state complete is forbidden.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FCore%20interface%20migration",
          "start_line":1315
        }
      ],
      "key":"core_migration_order",
      "statement":"Change the authoritative contract first, prohibit authoritative bridge code for its family, audit and migrate every producer/consumer, and chase every downstream effect until no superseded closure law remains. Complete migration-closure proof comes after the completed migration wave. Split producer contracts, inconsistent runtime/reporting/topology carriers, authoritative aliases or second-truth projections cannot be declared complete despite green tests.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["migration_strategies"],
      "conditions":["Selecting a strategy for core-interface or major implementation replacement."],
      "exceptions":["The presumption does not require an unsafe or constitutionally inadmissible predecessor path, nor retention of internal/public mechanisms explicitly superseded by product authority."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1361,
          "quote":"Where bounded evolution and fundamental re-adoption are both lawful and\nfeasible, and a working predecessor can reach the admitted product outcome\nwithout retaining competing or ambiguous authority, evolution on the\nsupported product path is the rebuttable selection presumption. This does\nnot require continuing an unsafe or constitutionally inadmissible\npredecessor path; it conserves accepted predecessor semantics and product\nfeedback, and it does not require preserving an internal implementation or\npublic mechanism that product authority explicitly supersedes. When bounded\nevolution includes a core-interface migration, it uses the inside-out\nhard-break strategy; bounded evolution is a strategy-selection presumption,\nnot a third strategy.\n\nFundamental re-adoption requires explicit selection by the human authority\nowning the affected product boundary. The selection compares re-adoption\nwith bounded evolution across product value, feedback latency, authority\nrisk, reversibility, total cost and retained predecessor semantics, and\nstates a bounded abort or re-entry condition. Implementation scale or\narchitectural preference alone does not select re-adoption (P6, P2).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FConstitutional%20migration%20options",
          "start_line":1344
        }
      ],
      "key":"migration_strategy_selection",
      "statement":"Prefer bounded evolution rebuttably only when both strategies are lawful/feasible and a working predecessor reaches the admitted outcome without competing/ambiguous authority. Core-interface evolution uses inside-out hard-break. Fundamental re-adoption requires explicit affected-product human-owner selection comparing product value, feedback latency, authority risk, reversibility, total cost and retained predecessor semantics, with bounded abort/re-entry condition. Scale or architectural preference alone cannot select it.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["constitutional_migration","acceptance_path","selected_product_outcome"],
      "conditions": [],
      "exceptions":["Residual superseded material outside the promoted path may be deferred only under all stated conditions."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1399,
          "quote":"Product-slice promotion and complete migration closure are distinct claims.\nAn intermediate product outcome may be promoted while its enclosing\nmigration remains open only when the outcome's acceptance path has singular\nauthority and the residual implementation satisfies the deferment rule\nabove. That promotion neither closes the migration nor waives its final\nretirement criteria. The slice's identity and acceptance criteria derive\nfrom the product plus every causally applicable live requirement, accepted\ndesign relation and retained predecessor claim; ticket or review wording\ncannot narrow that authority away. Active migration status does not renew\nan accepted slice's exhausted growth authority: work that only discharges\nalready admitted removal, demotion and migration-closure proof obligations\nmay continue within that existing bound without claiming further product\nprogress; further material producer or consumer growth requires another\nstill-live basis admitted under §3, and ticket activity alone is not that\nbasis.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FConstitutional%20migration%20options",
          "start_line":1385
        }
      ],
      "key":"path_relative_promotion",
      "statement":"Both strategies prohibit proxy partial interfaces standing for the new contract in any acceptance path and silently authoritative bridges/fallbacks. Promotion requires singular authority over the full causal closure, not one successful trace; old/new admissibility for the same request/fact/identity/state/projection is competing authority and blocks it. Outside-path residuals may be deferred only when explicitly non-authoritative for the outcome or governing explicitly specified disjoint product/compatibility scope with deterministic routing, unable to falsify current/retained claims, and carrying bounded migration/qualification/release disposition. Tests detect leaks/prove, not plan migration. An intermediate slice can promote only with singular path authority and compliant residuals, leaving migration visibly open and retirement criteria intact. Slice identity/criteria include product plus every causally applicable live requirement, accepted design and retained predecessor claim; ticket/review wording cannot narrow them. Active migration does not renew accepted-slice growth; already-admitted removal/demotion/closure proof may continue within its bound without new progress claims, while producer/consumer growth requires another live admitted basis.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["constitutional_migration"],
      "conditions":["Every core-interface migration."],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1422,
          "quote":"Every core interface migration explicitly declares: the exact affected\nmigration scope; every explicitly excluded or disjoint product or\ncompatibility scope and its deterministic routing relation; the new\nauthoritative contract; the superseded contract or surface; the\nauthoritative closure law for the new contract; the full set of producers\nof the old and new contract; the full set of consumers of the old and new\ncontract; and every projection, report, status surface and proof surface\nthat derives from it. Every old path is then classified as `remove`,\n`replace`, `re-authorize` or `temporary scaffolding`. Temporary scaffolding\nis lawful only if it is explicitly non-authoritative and scheduled for\nremoval before closure (P6, P4b).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FRequired%20migration%20protocol",
          "start_line":1412
        }
      ],
      "key":"migration_inventory_protocol",
      "statement":"Declare exact affected scope; every excluded/disjoint product or compatibility scope with deterministic routing; new authoritative and superseded contracts/surfaces; new closure law; full old/new producer and consumer sets; and every derived projection/report/status/proof surface. Classify every old path remove, replace, re-authorize or temporary scaffolding. Temporary scaffolding is lawful only when explicitly non-authoritative and scheduled for removal before closure.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["constitutional_migration"],
      "conditions":["A core-interface migration."],
      "exceptions":["An explicit live-product compatibility feature may retain a deliberately specified, bounded path clearly identified as compatibility rather than current native truth."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1435,
          "quote":"For a core interface migration: no compatibility alias remains\nauthoritative; no fallback identity law remains as silent runtime\nbehaviour; no bridge path participates in acceptance as if it were the new\ncontract; no old reader or writer remains authoritative for the promoted\noutcome once the new contract governs that outcome; no proxy or partial\nimplementation of the new interface stands between old and new truth as if\nit were completion. The only lawful exception is an explicit compatibility\nfeature retained as part of the live product, whose compatibility path is\nintentionally specified, explicitly bounded, and clearly identified as\ncompatibility rather than current native truth (P3, P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FBridge%20prohibition",
          "start_line":1426
        }
      ],
      "key":"bridge_authority_prohibition",
      "statement":"No authoritative compatibility alias, silent fallback identity law, bridge acceptance as new contract, old authoritative reader/writer for a newly governed promoted outcome, or proxy/partial interface pretending completion is lawful, subject only to the explicit compatibility-feature exception.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["projection_read_model","constitutional_migration"],
      "conditions": [],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1446,
          "quote":"If runtime, reporting, topology and proof\ncan disagree about closure because they consume different carriers or\ndifferent closure laws, the migration is incomplete; if a projection can\nindependently close while the authoritative carrier is still open, the\nmigration is incomplete; if the authoritative carrier can close while a\nprojection still depends on an older law, the migration is incomplete.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FProjection%20discipline",
          "start_line":1441
        }
      ],
      "key":"projection_closure_consistency",
      "statement":"Migration remains incomplete if runtime/reporting/topology/proof can disagree because of different carriers or closure laws, if a projection independently closes while its authoritative carrier is open, or if the carrier closes while a projection still depends on older law. Projections must not become second closure authority.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["constitutional_migration","migration_strategies"],
      "conditions":["Inside-out hard-break migration."],
      "exceptions":["Downstream exploration may exist as isolated non-authoritative work."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1463,
          "quote":"Inside-out hard-break migrations proceed from the new authoritative source\ncarrier outward (P4b, P6). The full\nbest-guess interface family is discovered before proof is used as closure\nevidence, including producers, consumers, projections, prompts, reports,\nwrappers, replay paths, ingest paths, bootstrap paths and proof surfaces.\nAuthoritative producer and source-carrier work comes before downstream\nconsumer, projection, prompt, dossier, report or review-surface hardening.\nDownstream exploration may exist only as isolated non-authoritative work\nand does not land in public runtime, projection, prompt, report or proof\nentry points until the source carrier is published and admitted. A\ndownstream projection ticket does not close while the source-carrier\nticket it depends on is still open. Dependency direction reflects this\norder explicitly so the ticket set exposes the migration wave from source\ntruth to downstream read models.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FInside-out%20first%20sequencing",
          "start_line":1450
        }
      ],
      "key":"inside_out_source_first",
      "statement":"Discover the full best-guess family before closure use of proof, including producers/consumers/projections/prompts/reports/wrappers/replay/ingest/bootstrap/proof surfaces. Authoritative producers and source carrier precede downstream consumer/projection/prompt/dossier/report/review hardening. Isolated exploration cannot land in public runtime/projection/prompt/report/proof entrypoints until source publication and admission. A projection ticket cannot close before its depended-on source-carrier ticket; explicit dependency direction exposes source-to-read-model order.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["constitutional_migration"],
      "conditions":["Each break in an inside-out interface-family migration."],
      "exceptions":["Explicitly specified, deterministically routed, non-overlapping product/compatibility scopes may coexist while migration stays open.","Newly discovered affected interfaces leave the same wave only through explicit upward repricing."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1483,
          "quote":"For each break: publish or admit the new deepest authoritative source\ncarrier; deliberately sever one old authoritative seam; keep that seam\nbroken; repair outward from source truth to consumers, then projections,\nthen prompts and reports, then proof surfaces; run negative proof that the\nsevered seam is rejected rather than merely unused. During the sequence,\ntests may discover missed interfaces but do not replace the required\ninterface inventory; newly discovered affected interfaces remain part of\nthe same migration wave unless the work is explicitly repriced upward;\nre-enabling the old seam through wrappers, fallbacks, projections or prompt\npaths is a migration defect.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FHard-break%20discipline",
          "start_line":1474
        }
      ],
      "key":"hard_break_sequence",
      "statement":"The wave is an ordered set of hard breaks, not permission for competing dual truth over the same request/fact/identity/state/projection. For each break publish or admit the deepest new carrier, sever and keep broken an old authoritative seam, repair outward through consumers, projections, prompts/reports and proof surfaces, then negatively prove rejection rather than mere nonuse. Tests may find interfaces but cannot replace the inventory. Re-enabling the seam via wrappers, fallback, projection or prompts is a defect.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["constitutional_migration"],
      "conditions": [],
      "exceptions":["Bounded slice proof may establish only the exact intermediate outcome allowed by path-relative promotion."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1495,
          "quote":"Complete migration-closure proof is not valid while producers and\nconsumers are split across old and new contracts. Bounded product-slice\nproof may establish only the exact intermediate outcome permitted by the\npath-relative promotion rule; it cannot establish migration closure (P6). Tests that pass only\nbecause bridge-state semantics remain alive do not count as closure proof;\ngreen local tests do not overrule a split architecture; migration-closure\nproof belongs after migration, not during a partially migrated state;\nper-break proof shows the severed old seam is rejected or fails closed\nbefore downstream hardening can count as progress.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FProof-last%20rule",
          "start_line":1487
        }
      ],
      "key":"proof_last_validity",
      "statement":"Complete migration proof is invalid while producers/consumers remain split across contracts; bridge-dependent passing tests and green local tests cannot establish closure. Slice proof is not migration proof. Per-break rejection/fail-closed evidence must precede counting downstream hardening as progress.",
      "uncertainty":""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["constitutional_migration"],
      "conditions":["Claiming completion across the declared affected scope."],
      "exceptions":["A superseded mechanism may remain for a distinct supported responsibility satisfying the retention burden."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1522,
          "quote":"A core interface migration is complete across its declared affected scope\nonly when every authoritative producer writes the new contract; every\nauthoritative consumer reads the new contract; superseded authoritative\npaths are retired unless justified for a distinct supported responsibility\nunder the retention relation below; projections are downstream of the same\nauthoritative truth rather than competing truth surfaces; runtime,\nreporting, topology and proof share one closure law; and no temporary\nscaffolding remains in the acceptance path. Until those conditions hold the\nwork remains an active migration wave, not a completed refactor (P3, P6).\n\nRetention bears the burden. Each retained superseded mechanism identifies a\ncurrent supported obligation, owner, purpose, execution scope and material\ncost. Temporary compatibility or comparison work has an expiry or removal\ntrigger; existing tests, an oracle label or nominal demotion do not justify\nduplicate production responsibility. A required cold reconstruction route\nor independent test oracle may serve an enduring distinct obligation\nwithout an invented expiry. Where an obligation survives replacement, its\nsurviving owner is identified; where it has no governing basis, no\nreplacement guard is required. Retirement inside a closed affected cone\ndoes not await unrelated whole-product closure (P2).\n\nAn accepted intermediate product slice within the wave names the slice it\ncloses and keeps the enclosing migration visibly open; it does not project\nslice acceptance as migration, qualification or release closure.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F7.%20Migration%2FClosure%20criterion",
          "start_line":1499
        }
