    {
      "allowed":["Projects choose different declared risk appetites and ambiguity policies.","Policy may permit bounded probabilistic carrying/decision or require escalation to admitted human adjudication.","Progressive reduction, rather than elimination of all ambiguity before work, is permitted."],
      "applies_to_keys":["ambiguity_governance","lawful_probabilistic_processing","human_adjudication"],
      "forbidden":["Silent convenience as the threshold.","Unrecorded ambiguity or decision.","Waiving ACTIVATION hard stops through risk appetite.","Treating carried, escalated or blocked ambiguity as resolved."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1234,
          "quote":"The default governance model: ambiguity\nmay be carried or decided by lawful probabilistic processing when project\npolicy allows it; it may be escalated to explicit human adjudication when\nproject policy requires it; the threshold between those actions is\ndetermined by declared risk appetite, not by silent convenience. Projects\nmay therefore choose different ambiguity-handling policies: a lower risk\nappetite escalates more major ambiguity to explicit human judgment; a\nhigher one permits more bounded probabilistic decision-making. In either\ncase the ambiguity and the decision are recorded.\n\nThe hard stops of [ACTIVATION §4](ACTIVATION.md#4-totality-and-refinement)\nhold regardless of risk appetite. The method is therefore not \"eliminate\nall ambiguity before work\"; it is \"make ambiguity visible, govern it\nexplicitly, and reduce it progressively until downstream realisation is\nsufficiently constrained\".",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F5.%20Ambiguity%20governance%20and%20lifecycle",
          "start_line":1220
        }
      ],
      "key":"ambiguity_policy_latitude"
    },
    {
      "allowed":["adrs/ is a default scaffold, not mandatory.","Default tenant-local location is build_tenants/<tenant-path>/design/adrs/; shared location build_tenants/common/design/adrs/; project/non-tenanted location <governing-design-surface>/adrs/.","Tenant paths may use multiple segments <product-family>/<realization-variant> (recommended default) or a single <realization-variant>; existing/alternative layouts remain conformant without migration.","Numeric IDs are default; namespace-prefixed IDs are allowed where already used by the owning design surface.","REGISTRY.md alongside ADRs is recommended but optional."],
      "applies_to_keys":["adr"],
      "forbidden":["Moving authority from ADR files to a registry.","Placing ADRs in comments, generated views, runtime archives or requirement folders.","Treating a default or recommendation as a mandatory migration."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1753,
          "quote":"ADRs are stored at the design surface that owns the decision; an `adrs/`\nsubdirectory is the default scaffold, not a mandatory location (P3). Tenant-local build-tenant ADRs go to the\napplicable tenant design surface bound by the product definition, by\ndefault `build_tenants/<tenant-path>/design/adrs/`; shared or cross-tenant\nADRs to the applicable common surface, by default\n`build_tenants/common/design/adrs/`; non-tenanted or project-local ADRs to\n`<governing-design-surface>/adrs/`. In the default scaffold `<tenant-path>`\nis one or more path segments beneath `build_tenants/`; both\ncapability-oriented `<product-family>/<realization-variant>` paths and\nsingle-label `<realization-variant>` paths are lawful, the former is the\nrecommended default, existing or alternative layouts remain conformant and\nneed no migration, and in every layout the product's tenant registry entry\nrecords the tenant identity and locations used (PRODUCT_DEFINITION §1).\nThe governing design surface is the closest design surface whose authority\nowns the decision. ADRs are not placed in comments, generated views,\nruntime archives or requirement folders. The filename convention is\n`ADR-<local-id>-short-slug.md`, the local identifier unique within the\nowning `adrs/` directory; numeric identifiers such as `ADR-001-...` are the\ndefault, and namespace-prefixed identifiers such as `ADR-GM-005-...` are\nallowed when the owning design surface already uses them. A `REGISTRY.md`\nindex alongside the ADR files is recommended for tooling and review and is\nnot required: the constitutional source remains each ADR file, and a\nregistry is a read model that may drift unless tooling maintains it.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F10.%20Design%20records",
          "start_line":1731
        }
      ],
      "key":"adr_layout_latitude"
    },
    {
      "allowed":["A conforming tenant may use software, human process or both.","A generic procedure requiring neither code, event stream nor complete-history reconstruction may use adequate human process with current records and direct observation.","Two conforming tenants may differ outside their contract.","Calculus reuse is permitted when each binding satisfies and demonstrates its laws.","Project law or tenant design selects multi-tenant development/merge mechanics."],
      "applies_to_keys":["build_tenant","build_tenant_binding","execution_environment"],
      "forbidden":["Selecting an environment that removes required human decisions or independent assessments.","Requiring code, event streams or complete-history reconstruction merely because the procedure is generic.","Treating executor identity, participation, convenience or structural validation as authority/conformance.","Weakening WHAT as a tenant-local choice."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1791,
          "quote":"- A generic procedure is a method procedure whose scope is independent of\n  the selected execution environment. It and its projections preserve the\n  bindings Rule 2 permits: code, an event stream or reconstruction from a\n  complete history is required only where the governing obligations or\n  the selected execution binding require it, and an adequate human\n  process using current records and direct observation is a permitted\n  execution binding of a generic procedure that requires none of them\n  (P1, P6).",
